\title{adaptive2in1count: 2-in-1 Adaptive Designs for Overdispersed Count Endpoints}
\description{
Evaluates the operating characteristics of a two-group 2-in-1 adaptive design whose Phase 2 and Phase 3 parts use overdispersed count endpoints, such as the exacerbation count over a short window and the annualized exacerbation rate in asthma and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease. Counts follow an additive gamma frailty model, so each endpoint is marginally negative binomial with its own dispersion, and the two endpoints of a patient are correlated both through the frailty and through the follow-up period they share. A stated proportion of patients may leave before the end of that period, with the period distributed as in Tang (2015) \doi{10.1080/10543406.2014.971167}. Final analyses are Wald tests of the log rate ratio from a negative binomial model with an offset for the realized exposure, and the type I error rate, the power, the probability of expansion, the expected sample size and the required sample size follow from the resulting trivariate normal distribution. Simulation and an exact evaluation under complete follow-up are provided for checking. The design follows the 2-in-1 framework of Chen, Anderson, Mehrotra, Rubin and Tse (2018) \doi{10.1016/j.cct.2017.09.006}.
}
